WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  think back to the first time when you  became aware of a deep injustice  do you remember what that was mine was  with grandpa lou  he was 80 years old had a history of  asthma and so when he woke up in the  middle of the night with chest pains  there was cause for concern a small  ambulance arrived and took him away to  the nearest hospital  there the on-staff nurse brought in the  only doctor who spoke mandarin  grandpaloo tried to explain what was  happening in his native language  but the doctor didn't see anything  urgent and left  grandpalu had no idea what was happening  in my community being born in the u.s  and speaking  native english opens doors i recognize  from a young age the obligation  that came with my fluency if i could  open doors with my english  it was also my job to hold the door open  for others  my mom always told me you are lucky you  don't have an accent you don't speak  with bad grammar  they will listen to you  and so as a kid i remember helping  people apply for visas  handling problems at the dmv and even  writing angry letters to their landlords  i've even pretended to deepen my voice  to call the bank as my father  but i was in college when grandpalou was  in the hospital only getting updates  from thousands of miles away  my mom and another family friend rushed  to the hospital to help grandpalou in  case there were any translation issues  now keep in mind neither my mom nor this  family friend are particularly  comfortable with english  but they were all grandpa lou had  during one of my mom's so-called  translation shifts  the family friend told her that they had  taken grandpa lou away to surgery  to remove his appendix my mom was  stunned  grandpa lou seemed to be recovering the  last time she was there  and appendicitis seemed unrelated to the  chest pains he was experiencing  nobody had any idea why the surgery was  necessary  or what was happening  it's unclear if the doctors even  understood that grandpalu had asthma  grandpa lou never recovered he never  left the hospital  he died within a week  back then none of us realized that  federal law requires  all patients to have access to  healthcare in their preferred language  back then none of us realized that  grampalu could have asked for an  interpreter  even though interpreters are sometimes  hard to find and sometimes lack the  right training  all we knew was that grandpa lou did not  speak english  and the american healthcare system  failed him  and let's remember english is not the  official language of the u.s  i'll repeat that the united states  legally  does not have an official language  and certain language barriers in  situations like health care  are considered civil rights violations  due to discrimination based on  national origin you see even back in  colonial times  our founding fathers recognized that the  united states is composed of a symphony  of voices  and to exclude any of them would be  undemocratic  and a threat to individual liberty  give me your tired your poor your  huddled masses yearning to breathe free  nowhere in the american dream does it  say  but leave your foreign tongues and heavy  accents at the door  according to the pew research center 67  million americans speak a language  other than english at home that's one in  five americans  we need an america that is language  inclusive  for too long non-native english speakers  have been othered  in my own asian american community i've  heard the internalized mantra  we're in their country we need to change  but for the millions of us who have made  this country our home  isn't this our country too  language is a core aspect of our  identity  yet the hegemony of the english language  has made life more difficult for  millions  across income level and educational  status  and let's remember this is not just an  immigration issue  the historical erasure of native  american languages is a tragedy  and yet another example of linguistic  injustice in the u.s  now if you're from a non-native  english-speaking household like me  you might have similar stories we've  never had the luxury  of not caring about linguistic justice  yet i've realized that many in this  nation including those in the business  world  don't really know what's going on  i've spent my career in healthcare and  impact investing  i met with countless founders who  excitedly pitch me their ideas  that will increase access and  inclusivity  yet very rarely if ever do i hear these  founders talk about  language inclusivity  in the realm of healthcare language  access has been shown to improve patient  satisfaction  increase use of preventative services  and lead to overall better health  outcomes  over the last nine months i've been  working on a startup idea to help bridge  the gap  in linguistic justice in healthcare and  to give family members like myself  better tools to assist their loved ones  with their healthcare journey  i've interviewed over 150 patients and  healthcare providers and when i talk to  doctors  many of them apologetically tell me that  they aren't able to spend as much time  with their non-native english-speaking  patients  others have gone so far as to admit that  language services at their hospitals are  poor  because they know this population isn't  litigious  others have admitted to taking care  instructions putting them in google  translate  and then sending their patients on their  way home  now machine translation holds enormous  potential  but it is nowhere good enough in  situations like healthcare  where complete understanding is required  despite the growth of digital health  tools during this pandemic  health inequity has worsened  take the coveted vaccine for example in  my home county  coveted vaccine registration forms are  only available in english and spanish  neither of which are my parents  preferred language  in la county one of the most diverse  areas in the u.s  their coveted vaccine information is  only available in translated forms  through google translate  all of my friends who have loved ones  who don't speak english  have been struggling to figure out how  to register their family for this  vaccine  i built a website to help bring together  this information  and to provide loved ones with a better  tool to share information with their  family in their preferred language  we saw the need for this in our own  families  and i want to help open doors for the  millions of families like mine  so i'll say it again we need an america  that is language inclusive  this problem is solvable  there are laws in place to support  language access  but it's often up to the private sector  to execute upon these policies  you see the business world has a key  role to play in linguistic justice  companies can all consider  multilingualism in the design of their  products and services  and bring in people from these  communities to build more trust  and connection of doing the bare minimum  companies can be at the forefront of  innovation that truly  broadens access  i want an america where my family  can easily access public health  information  i want an america where grandpa lou  could have understand exactly what was  happening when he was wheeled away to  surgery  i want an america where i can leave the  room  and know that my loved ones will get  treated exactly the same  we all need an america that is language  inclusive  so talk about this problem ask questions  about whether your company  considers language access in the design  of its products and services  develop and invest in solutions that  open doors  instead of ones that keep doors closed  and to the millions of american families  like mine  i hear you and i will open the door for  you  you

